/* ============================================================================
   combinations.epher -- unordered selections: ncr(n, r) and committees

   Counts unordered selections with the ncr builtin (guide 1.13): the
   number of ways to choose r distinct objects out of n when the order
   does not matter. The classic setting: choosing a committee of 3 from
   10 people, where the group is the same no matter how its members are
   listed, gives ncr(10, 3) = 120 committees.

   Cross-checks: ncr(n, r) = ncr(n, n - r) (choosing the 3 members is
   choosing the 7 left out), ncr(n, r) = npr(n, r) / r! (each committee
   is counted r! times among ordered lists), and the hockey-stick
   identity ncr(n, r) = ncr(n - 1, r - 1) + ncr(n - 1, r). The counts are
   exact integers; the sums are accumulated in a packed while loop.

   Source: Ross, "A First Course in Probability", chapter 1.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/counting/combinations.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\counting\combinations.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/counting/combinations.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter

   The knob to edit is `group`; everything below it is machinery.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ----
const group = 10

// ---- results ----
// Committees of 3 from `group` people; choosing the 3 members is the
// same choice as naming the 7 people left out.
print("committees of 3 from", group, "people:", ncr(group, 3))
print("the same choice as 7 left out:", ncr(group, 7))
ncr(group, 3) == ncr(group, 7)

// The formula link to permutations: every 3-person committee appears in
// 3! = 6 orders among the npr(10, 3) ordered lists.
ncr(group, 3) == npr(group, 3) / fact(3)

// Hockey-stick identity, with exact integers.
ncr(9, 2) + ncr(9, 3) == ncr(10, 3)

// Two famous counts that reuse combinations.
print("5-card hands from a 52-card deck:", ncr(52, 5))
print("lottery tickets, 6 of 49 numbers:", ncr(49, 6))

// Row 5 of Pascal's triangle sums to 2^5 = 32: accumulate with a packed
// while loop (guide 1.30), since a sum of a collected list is not a
// builtin operation.
v = {0, 0}
while v[1] < 6 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + ncr(5, v[1])}
print("row 5 of Pascal's triangle sums to:", v[2])
print("and 2^5 =", 2 ^ 5)
v[2] == 2 ^ 5
/* ---- expected output ----
= 10
= committees of 3 from 10 people: 120
= the same choice as 7 left out: 120
= true
= true
= true
= 5-card hands from a 52-card deck: 2598960
= lottery tickets, 6 of 49 numbers: 13983816
= {0, 0}
= row 5 of Pascal's triangle sums to: 32
= and 2^5 = 32
= true
*/
